Training data: Internet Archive scans
-------------------------------------

Training lines were also cut from these Internet Archive items
(https://archive.org/details/<identifier>), scans of public-domain books.
They were fetched from the Internet Archive (its metadata and IIIF
services), the route HeOCR's legal strategy calls internet_archive. The
Internet Archive publishes them without an explicit licence: none carries a
licenseurl or rights field. The original conditions of each item on the
Internet Archive still apply. None comes from NLI.

Each item passed HeOCR's Internet Archive rights gate
(hocrgen.fetchers.ia_iiif.decide_ia_rights; hocrgen decision
planning/decisions/2026-11-09-internet-archive-library-scans.md), and each
Ben-Yehuda work aligned to it has its own rights justification
(hocrgen.rights_justification.v1; hocrgen decision
planning/decisions/2026-11-10-legal-strategy.md). In short: a public-domain
book, a faithful scan, fetched from the Internet Archive; no new right
arises in the scan and no contract binds its reuse. The justification ids
are listed in r8b_training_run.json. Death years are the ones those
justifications record, with one exception: Alfred Dreyfus died in 1935
(Wikidata), but his justification conservatively records 1939, the Internet
Archive's creator dates. Either year puts the book in the public domain.

